//? Title: Halley root finding: a rational step and its limits
//? Subtitle: Actual callbacks, local cubic convergence and honest floating residuals
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given a scalar function and its first two derivatives, find an input where the
function is zero. Derivative information can accelerate a nearby search, but an
unbracketed iteration can stall or fail, and a small residual need not mean
small coordinate error. This report derives Halley's rational update, follows
actual core callback records for the original square-root-of-two example,
compares producing Newton evaluations, and tests repeated roots and numerical
stopping controls. Cubic convergence is a local mathematical property, rather
than a promise of three times as many correct digits at every floating step.
)

\toc

> Check the first step by hand

For \(f(x)=x^2-2\) from x=2, the actual values are f=2, f′=4 and f″=2. The
denominator is 2·4²−2·2=28, so the correction is 16/28=4/7 and the next iterate
is 10/7. Its exact residual is 2/49. The following complete program takes that
update through the shared core; its second inspection evaluates the resulting
rounded iterate. The legacy iteration count is one here because the update
budget is one, while two calls to f are needed to check the final result.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/halley_step.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto f = [](double x) { return x * x - 2.; };
    auto df = [](double x) { return 2 * x; };
    auto d2 = [](double) { return 2.; };

    const auto t = dm::algorithms::halley_trace(f, df, d2, 2., 1e-10, 1);
    const auto &a = t.steps.at(0);
    std::print("| inspection | x | f(x) | derivatives evaluated | next | update |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | " "--- | ---: | --- |\n");
    for (const auto &v : t.steps)
        std::print("| {} | {:.17g} | {:.17g} | {} | {:.17g} | {} |\n",
                   v.inspection, v.x, v.fx, v.derivatives_evaluated, v.next,
                   v.updated);
    std::print("\nActual denominator {}; f calls {}; updates {}; legacy iterations {}; converged {}.\n",
               a.denominator, t.run.work.f_calls, t.run.work.updates,
               t.run.result.iterations, t.run.result.converged);
    dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(65, 1100);
    dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle style;
    style.width = 4;
    style.height = 1.8;
    style.font_size = 22;
    diagram.node("values", {0, 0},
                 std::format("x = {}\nf = {}, f′ = {}, f″ = {}", a.x, a.fx,
                             a.derivative, a.second_derivative), style).node(
        "denominator", {6, 0}, std::format("2 f′² − f f″\n= {}", a.denominator),
        style).node("next", {12, 0},
                    std::format("x − 2 f f′ / denominator\n= {:.9f}", a.next),
                    style).edge("values", "denominator").edge("denominator",
                                                              "next").text(
        {6, 2}, "One actual Halley update: 2 → 10/7", 25).save_svg(
        "build/halley_step.svg");
    if (a.denominator != 28 || a.next != 2. - 16. / 28 ||
        t.run.work.f_calls != 2)
        throw std::runtime_error("first step");
}
```
