| inspection | x | f(x) | derivatives evaluated | next | update |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | --- | ---: | --- |
| 1 | 2 | 2 | true | 1.4285714285714286 | true |
| 2 | 1.4285714285714286 | 0.04081632653061229 | false | 1.4285714285714286 | false |

Actual denominator 28; f calls 2; updates 1; legacy iterations 1; converged false.
\image(figures/halley_step.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual callback values feed the denominator and next iterate. The final inspection uses no derivatives and makes no update.)
\alttext(Three nodes show the evaluated function and derivatives, denominator 28 and rounded next iterate 10/7.)

> Derive the rational update and its local order

At a current point x, let t be a proposed displacement and match f, f′ and f″
with the rational function

$$R(t)=f(x)+\frac{f'(x)t}{1-ct},\qquad c=\frac{f''(x)}{2f'(x)}.$$
\alttext(A rational function with a linear numerator and denominator matches the value and first two derivatives at displacement zero, where the first derivative is nonzero.)

Its Taylor coefficients through t² agree with those of f(x+t). Setting its
numerator to zero gives

$$t=-\frac{2ff'}{2(f')^2-ff''},\qquad x_{next}=x+t.$$
\alttext(The rational interpolant's zero supplies the Halley correction, provided its update denominator is nonzero.)

The rational construction assumes f′≠0. The implemented algebraic formula can
still be evaluated with f′=0 and a nonzero denominator, in which case it applies
a zero correction and can stall. There is no bracketing safeguard or automatic
Newton fallback.

For a sufficiently smooth simple root α, expand f, f′ and f″ at α and substitute
into that quotient. The constant and linear terms cancel in the next error, as
does its quadratic term. With e=x−α,

$$e_{next}=\left[\frac{f''(\alpha)^2}{4f'(\alpha)^2}-\frac{f^{(3)}(\alpha)}{6f'(\alpha)}\right]e^3+O(e^4).$$
\alttext(The leading local error is cubic for a smooth simple root with nonzero first derivative. Its coefficient depends on the derivatives at that root.)

For x²−2 the exact maps simplify further. With r=√2,

$$H(x)=\frac{x(x^2+6)}{3x^2+2},\quad H(x)-r=\frac{(x-r)^3}{3x^2+2},\quad
N(x)-r=\frac{(x-r)^2}{2x}.$$
\alttext(The square-root problem has exact cubic and quadratic error identities for Halley and Newton respectively, away from their denominators' zeros.)

These identities explain the local orders without fitting a line to a few
rounded observations. They do not make derivative evaluation free, prove
convergence from an arbitrary start, or overcome floating cancellation.

> Run the original square-root comparison

Both solvers start at two and use their actual residual stopping rule with
tolerance 1e−15. The Newton curve records inputs to its real f callback, rather
than running a second version of Newton's formula. Halley's curve uses the
producing core trace. The plotted quantity is the actual absolute callback
residual; values of exactly zero would be omitted from the log plot and printed
in the table, without an invented plotting floor. Residual is in function units,
and near a simple root it approximates coordinate error multiplied by |f′(α)|.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/halley_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto f = [](double x) { return x * x - 2.; };
    auto df = [](double x) { return 2 * x; };
    auto d2 = [](double) { return 2.; };

    const auto h = dm::algorithms::halley_trace(f, df, d2, 2., 1e-15, 20);
    std::vector<double> hx, hy, nx, ny;
    std::size_t nf = 0, nd = 0;
    std::print("| method | f inspection | x | actual absolute residual |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (const auto &a : h.steps) {
        std::print("| Halley | {} | {:.17g} | {:.17g} |\n", a.inspection, a.x,
                   std::abs(a.fx));
        if (a.fx != 0) {
            hx.push_back(a.inspection - 1);
            hy.push_back(std::log10(std::abs(a.fx)));
        }
    }
    auto n = dm::algorithms::newton_raphson([&](double x) {
        auto y =
            f(x); std::print("| Newton | {} | {:.17g} | {:.17g} |\n", nf + 1, x,
                             std::abs(y)); if (y != 0) {
            nx.push_back(nf); ny.push_back(std::log10(std::abs(y)));
        }
        ++ nf; return y;
    }, [&](double x) {
        ++nd; return df(x);
    }, 2., 1e-15, 20);
    std::print("\nHalley converged {}; legacy iterations {}; applied updates {}; f/df/d2f calls {}/{}/{}. " "Newton converged {}; legacy iterations {}; f/df calls {}/{}.\n",
               h.run.result.converged, h.run.result.iterations,
               h.run.work.updates, h.run.work.f_calls, h.run.work.df_calls,
               h.run.work.d2f_calls, n.converged, n.iterations, nf, nd);
    auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    plot.line(hx, hy, "Halley", {37, 99, 235}, 2.).points(hx, hy, "",
                                                          {37, 99, 235}, 6.).line(nx, ny, "Newton", {220, 38, 38}, 2.).points(nx, ny, "", {220, 38, 38}, 6.).title("Original x² − 2: actual callback residuals").x_label("f inspection index, starting at zero").y_label("log10 |actual f(x)|").save_svg("build/halley_original.svg");
    if (!h.run.result.converged || !n.converged)
        throw std::runtime_error("original convergence");
}
```
