\caption(Actual residuals retain their floating values. Counts distinguish successful residual inspections from applied updates; fewer steps alone do not establish lower runtime.)
\alttext(Halley and Newton residual curves decrease at different rates before reaching the floating residual floor, using callbacks from the actual two solver executions.)

> A repeated root changes the order

Simple-root assumptions matter. For \(f(x)=x^m\), direct substitution gives

$$x_{next}=\frac{m-1}{m+1}x\qquad (m>1,\ x\ne0).$$
\alttext(At a root of multiplicity m greater than one, the ordinary Halley formula contracts linearly by the factor m minus one divided by m plus one.)

For m=3 the factor is one half. Starting at one gives exactly representable
dyadic iterates for the short run below. Tolerance zero disables strict residual
acceptance, so this block executes the requested eight updates and includes a
ninth, final f inspection. It establishes linear contraction for this example,
rather than an empirical general convergence theorem.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/halley_multiple.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto f = [](double x) { return x * x - 2.; };
    auto df = [](double x) { return 2 * x; };
    auto d2 = [](double) { return 2.; };

    const auto t = dm::algorithms::halley_trace([](double x) {
        return x * x * x;
    }, [](double x) {
        return 3 * x * x;
    }, [](double x) {
        return 6 * x;
    }, 1., 0., 8);
    std::vector<double> x, y;
    std::print("| inspection | iterate | residual | updated |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |\n");
    for (const auto &a : t.steps) {
        std::print("| {} | {:.12g} | {:.12g} | {} |\n", a.inspection, a.x, a.fx,
                   a.updated);
        x.push_back(a.inspection - 1);
        y.push_back(std::log2(std::abs(a.x)));
        if (a.x != std::ldexp(1., 1 - a.inspection))
            throw std::runtime_error("multiplicity contraction");
    }
    std::print("\nActual updates {}; f calls {}; converged {}.\n",
               t.run.work.updates, t.run.work.f_calls, t.run.result.converged);
    auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    plot.line(x, y, "actual x^3 iterates", {37, 99, 235}, 2.).points(
        x, y, "", {37, 99, 235}, 6.).title("Repeated root: error halves, rather than cubes").x_label("applied updates before inspection").y_label("log2 |x|, exact root zero").save_svg("build/halley_multiple.svg");
}
```
