| case | root estimate | residual | converged | stop enum | updates | f calls |
| --- | ---: | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| ordinary | 1.41421356237 | 4.4408920985e-16 | true | 0 | 3 | 4 |
| scaled | 2 | 2e-12 | true | 0 | 0 | 1 |
| zero denominator | 0 | 1 | false | 1 | 0 | 1 |
| zero correction | 0 | 1 | false | 2 | 5 | 6 |

Online cap one rejects before second f callback: true; actual f calls before rejection 1.
\image(figures/halley_controls.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Stop enums are Residual=0, ZeroDenominator=1 and IterationLimit=2. The scaled residual accepts a poor initial coordinate; the zero-correction case consumes its update budget.)
\alttext(Bars show three ordinary updates, zero scaled-function updates, zero zero-denominator updates and five repeated zero-correction updates.)

All callbacks and controls are validated before execution: callables must be
nonempty, x0 finite, tolerance finite and nonnegative, and the nonnegative
update budget below INT_MAX. Nonfinite callback output throws a domain error;
nonfinite denominator or correction arithmetic throws an overflow error.
Callback exceptions propagate. These checks improve failure handling without
changing the ordinary legacy update arithmetic. There is no claim that finite
arithmetic proves the supplied derivatives correct.

The legacy `iterations` field counts loop inspections, including a successful
initial residual check. With budget zero it stays zero, although f is evaluated
once. A zero denominator returns without an update. Budget exhaustion evaluates
f at the last updated x, and may still set converged if its strict residual
passes. With tolerance zero even a zero callback value fails that strict test.
There is no stagnation stop.

`halley_run` adds exact selected callback/update counts; `halley_trace` captures
those same inspected values and decisions from one execution. It admits one
whole record before each f callback. The default online cap is one million
records, zero disabling the policy cap; it is not bytes or peak memory. Failure
returns no partial trace, but earlier callback side effects remain. Initial
inputs, derivative outputs and next iterates are owned values with no retained
callable references. Calls share no internal mutable state.

For K updates that reach the budget check, there are K+1 f calls and K calls to
each derivative. Earlier residual or denominator stops can reduce those counts.
Runtime includes callback costs plus O(K) scalar arithmetic. The untraced solver
uses constant working storage; a trace adds one owned record per f call. The
extra derivative prevents inferring a runtime win from a shorter iteration
sequence alone.

> What this establishes

The original square-root example demonstrates fast local progress from a
favourable start. The exact error identities explain its cubic order without
invented error floors. The repeated-root control proves why the simple-root
condition matters, and the stopping controls distinguish a small function value
from an accurate coordinate. A dataset would not strengthen this scalar
numerical example.

The historical primary reference is Edmond Halley (1694), [A new, exact, and
easy method of finding the roots of equations generally, and that without any
previous reduction](https://doi.org/10.1098/rstl.1694.0029), Philosophical
Transactions 18, 136–148. This report gives a modern rational-interpolation
derivation and explicitly describes the library's residual and update-budget
policy.

Verification compares finite legacy results with a frozen pre-conversion
implementation, certifies actual callback order and trace records under
sanitizers, and uses independent high-precision arithmetic for square-root
updates and exact dyadic identities for the repeated-root sequence. API
examples, language-server descriptions, every complete report program and both
exports are checked before retiring the original report.
